<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sergei Eisenstein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1948">1948</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sergei Eisenstein was a Soviet film director and theorist whose use of montage made him one of the most influential figures in the history of cinema. His films include Battleship Potemkin (1925), October (1928), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> (1938), celebrating the victory on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-peipus">Lake Peipus</a>, and Ivan the Terrible, whose second part was banned by Stalin. His writings on film form remain widely studied.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sergei-eisenstein.jpg" alt="Sergei Eisenstein" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sergei_Eisenstein_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sergei Mikhailovich Eisenstein was born on 22 January 1898 in Riga, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, to a middle-class family. His father, the architect Mikhail Osipovich Eisenstein, came from a Jewish family but had converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> before his marriage in 1897. His mother took Sergei to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a> in 1905. He was raised an Orthodox Christian and later became an atheist. At the Petrograd Institute of Civil Engineering he studied architecture and engineering, but in 1918 he left to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in 1920 he saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">Kabuki</a> theatre and studied Japanese, learning about 300 kanji characters, which he cited as an influence on his pictorial development.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1920 Eisenstein moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and worked in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> for Proletkult, an experimental institution that aimed at a revolutionary working-class aesthetic. In 1923 he began as a theorist with the essay The Montage of Attractions. His first full-length film was Strike in 1925, followed in the same year by Battleship Potemkin, which was acclaimed around the world, and by October: Ten Days That Shook the World, made for the tenth anniversary of the Revolution. His attention to camera angles, crowd movements and montage drew criticism in the Soviet film community and led to self-criticism and promises to conform to socialist realism.</p><p>In 1930 Paramount Pictures gave him a short contract in Hollywood, but his projects failed to impress the studio and it ended by mutual consent. He then made a film in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, Que viva Mexico, financed by the author Upton Sinclair and other investors. It ran far beyond the three or four months agreed, and Sinclair shut down production, leaving the footage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where others edited it into films that Eisenstein never saw. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, made in 1938 with music by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergei-prokofiev">Sergei Prokofiev</a>, was received well in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the West and won him the Order of Lenin and the Stalin Prize.</p><p>During the war he worked on the film about Tsar Ivan IV, with Prokofiev scoring it. Ivan the Terrible, Part I won a Stalin Prize. Part II was criticised by various authorities and went unreleased until 1958, and the footage of the unfinished Part III was confiscated and mostly destroyed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eisenstein suffered a heart attack on 2 February 1946 and died of a second on 11 February 1948, aged 50. His ashes were buried in the Novodevichy Cemetery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. He was a pioneer in the theory and practice of montage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sergei Eisenstein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its defeats of Swedish and Livonian forces, especially Alexander Nevsky’s victories, entered Russian national memory and were celebrated in <b>Sergei Eisenstein</b>’s 1938 film.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Early Soviet culture produced an influential avant-garde, from Constructivist art and architecture to the films of <b>Sergei Eisenstein</b> and the poetry of Vladimir Mayakovsky, before Socialist Realism was imposed in the 1930s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet culture combined official socialist realism with world-class achievement in music, ballet, film and literature, from Sergei Prokofiev, Dmitri Shostakovich and <b>Sergei Eisenstein</b> to Boris Pasternak and Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, whose account of the Gulag shaped Western views of the system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Riga, in what is now <a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Riga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sergei Eisenstein"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4726 5249h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4726 5249h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.95,24.11&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
